As wordplay, the connection is sharp; etymologically, they differ.

Pray means to ask or entreat. Prey is something seized or hunted. They became homophones through language change, not shared origin.

Likewise, prediction and predation share the prefix pre- (“before”), but not the same root. Yet functionally, prediction can enable predation: anticipate movement, reduce uncertainty, intercept the target. The same capacity can enable protection—forecast danger, avoid contact, preserve life.

So prediction is power over possible futures. Whether it becomes predation, prayer, preparation, or protection depends on intent and conduct.